ompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"computer_name": {"description": "The folder's computer_name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}, "environment_id": {"description": "The folder's environment_id from list_computer_folders.", "type": "string"}, "folder": {"description": "The folder's name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["environment_id", "computer_name", "folder"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential", "schema": {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say 